                <pb facs="0359" n="187" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp01v01/pg187"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 857.857/112</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-03-24">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 24, 1917</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received 6 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1959. American consular agent, Brest, reports sinking on 15th instant at 8 p.m.,
                    of Norwegian steamer <hi rendition="#italic">Wilfred</hi>, from Newport,
                    England, to Gibraltar with coal. He obtained signed statement of two Americans,
                        Bill Southwood, born November 5, 1895, Wayne County,
                    Ky., coal trimmer (bearer of seaman’s identification certificate), and
                        John Palmer, born June 28, 1892, Panama (sailor’s
                    papers lost, signed on as American), who state that <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilfred</hi> did not carry troops and was not armed. German submarine was
                    seen; it allowed crew to take to boats. <hi rendition="#italic">Wilfred</hi>
                    displayed Norwegian flag and regular marks on both sides. Sank 20 miles from
                    Ushant, no vessel in sight. No other Americans on board. Crew reached shore next
                    morning, no casualties. Consular agent states sea was rough and weather cold;
                    his report and the signed statement follow by mail. He has been requested to
                    obtain additional information.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sharp</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
